steps.body:
To visit every character, loop over the indexes `0` to `length() - 1` and read each one with `charAt(i)`. A String uses `length()` with parentheses, unlike an array's `length` field.

```java
String word = "hello";
for (int i = 0; i < word.length(); i++) {
    char c = word.charAt(i);
    if (c == 'l') {
        System.out.println("l at index " + i);
    }
}
```

`charAt` returns a `char`, and `char` is a primitive, so `==` compares the actual characters. That is safe here, unlike `==` on Strings. A char literal uses single quotes: `'a'` is a `char`, while `"a"` is a String. Writing `c == "a"` is a compile error because a `char` and a String cannot be compared.

Use the index loop when you need `i`, for example to report where a character sits or to look at a neighbor with `charAt(i + 1)`. Keep the bound as `i < s.length()`: `charAt(s.length())` throws `StringIndexOutOfBoundsException`.

example.code:
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String sentence = "learning java one char at a time";

        int vowels = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < sentence.length(); i++) {
            char c = sentence.charAt(i);
            if (c == 'a' || c == 'e' || c == 'i' || c == 'o' || c == 'u') {
                vowels++;
            }
            if (c == 'a') {
                System.out.println("a at index " + i);
            }
        }
        System.out.println("Vowels: " + vowels);
    }
}

