steps.body:
When you only need the characters and not their positions, `toCharArray()` turns the String into a `char[]` you can walk with an enhanced for loop.

```java
for (char c : "Hi 5".toCharArray()) {
    if (Character.isLetter(c)) {
        System.out.print(Character.toUpperCase(c));
    }
}
```

The `Character` class answers questions about one `char`. Each test returns a `boolean`:

- `Character.isLetter(c)` and `Character.isDigit(c)`
- `Character.isUpperCase(c)` and `Character.isLowerCase(c)`
- `Character.isWhitespace(c)` is true for spaces, tabs, and newlines

`Character.toUpperCase(c)` and `Character.toLowerCase(c)` return a new `char` and leave anything that is not a letter unchanged. Like String methods, they do not change `c` itself, so use the return value.

Gotcha: `System.out.println(chars)` prints the characters of a `char[]`, but `"Chars: " + chars` prints a type and a hash like `[C@1b6d3586`. Use `String.valueOf(chars)` when you need the text inside a concatenation. Also note that `isLetter` is true for letters such as `'é'`, not only `a` to `z`.

example.code:
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String text = "Room 42 is on Floor 3";

        int letters = 0;
        int digits = 0;
        int spaces = 0;
        for (char c : text.toCharArray()) {
            if (Character.isLetter(c)) {
                letters++;
            } else if (Character.isDigit(c)) {
                digits++;
            } else if (Character.isWhitespace(c)) {
                spaces++;
            }
        }
        System.out.println("Letters: " + letters);
        System.out.println("Digits: " + digits);
        System.out.println("Spaces: " + spaces);

        for (char c : text.toCharArray()) {
            System.out.print(Character.toUpperCase(c));
        }
        System.out.println();
    }
}

