steps.body:
A `char` is a small number underneath: `'a'` is 97, `'b'` is 98, and the letters and digits are in order. That makes subtraction useful:

- `c - 'a'` turns `'a'` to `'z'` into an index from 0 to 25
- `c - '0'` turns `'0'` to `'9'` into the digit value 0 to 9
- `(char) ('a' + i)` turns an index back into a letter

```java
int[] counts = new int[26];
for (char c : "abba".toCharArray()) {
    counts[c - 'a']++;
}
System.out.println(counts[1]);        // 2, the count of 'b'
System.out.println('7' - '0');        // 7
System.out.println((char) ('a' + 2)); // c

char next = 'x';
next++;
System.out.println(next);             // y
```

Gotcha: arithmetic on a `char` produces an `int`. `System.out.println('a' + 1)` prints `98`, not `b`, so cast back with `(char)` when you want a letter. The increment forms are the exception: `c++` and `c += 1` keep a `char` a `char`, as `next++` shows.

`c - 'a'` only works for `'a'` to `'z'`. A space or an upper case letter gives an index outside 0 to 25 and throws `ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException`. Guard with a range check like `c >= 'a' && c <= 'z'`, not `Character.isLetter`, which is also true for `'A'` and `'é'`. For digits, check `c >= '0' && c <= '9'`.

example.code:
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String text = "banana split";

        int[] counts = new int[26];
        for (char c : text.toCharArray()) {
            if (c >= 'a' && c <= 'z') {
                counts[c - 'a']++;
            }
        }

        for (int i = 0; i < counts.length; i++) {
            if (counts[i] > 0) {
                char letter = (char) ('a' + i);
                System.out.println(letter + ": " + counts[i]);
            }
        }
    }
}

