steps.body:
To walk a String backwards, start at the last index and count down. The last index is `s.length() - 1`, and the loop stops after index 0.

```java
for (int i = s.length() - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
    System.out.print(s.charAt(i));
}
```

For checks that compare the two ends, use two indexes that move toward each other. `left` starts at 0, `right` at the last index, and the loop runs while `left < right`. The first mismatch settles the answer, so `break` there.

```java
boolean same = true;
int left = 0;
int right = s.length() - 1;
while (left < right) {
    if (s.charAt(left) != s.charAt(right)) {
        same = false;
        break;
    }
    left++;
    right--;
}
```

Gotchas: starting at `s.length()` instead of `s.length() - 1` throws `StringIndexOutOfBoundsException`. The middle character of an odd length word never needs checking, which is why `left < right` is enough. To ignore case, compare `Character.toLowerCase` of both chars, or lower-case the whole word once with `toLowerCase()`.

example.code:
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String word = "stressed";
        System.out.print(word + " backwards: ");
        for (int i = word.length() - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
            System.out.print(word.charAt(i));
        }
        System.out.println();

        String[] words = {"racecar", "rotator", "rocket"};
        for (String w : words) {
            boolean isPalindrome = true;
            int left = 0;
            int right = w.length() - 1;
            while (left < right) {
                if (w.charAt(left) != w.charAt(right)) {
                    isPalindrome = false;
                    break;
                }
                left++;
                right--;
            }
            System.out.println(w + " " + isPalindrome);
        }
    }
}

